# Conservative EAS Build exclusions (Build 9). Only excludes directories confirmed to have NO
# runtime import/require from src/ (grepped before adding each entry) -- never anything the
# Expo app actually bundles or needs at build time.
#
# CORRECTION (Build 9 correction pass): the original comment here claimed src/app/creature-lab.tsx
# imports src/data/creature-lab/creature-assets.ts -- that was wrong (a same-named-folder
# misread; the route only ever imported the separate, current src/data/creature-test/ system).
# The actual obsolete tree (src/data/creature-lab/, src/components/creature-lab/,
# scripts/creature-factory/, and the asset folders assets/creatures-canonical/,
# assets/creatures-dev-registered/, assets/creatures-dev-prototype/ it alone referenced) had
# zero real importers and has been git-rm'd entirely -- see the Build 9 correction report for
# before/after archive size. assets/creatures/ is the current production asset tree
# (used by CreatureAvatar for every real user) and is correctly never excluded here.
#
# CORRECTIVE NOTE (Work Package 3, Phase B Stage 2 corrective commit): a Stage 2 attempt to
# require() optimized .png siblings instead of the original .svg wrappers, and to exclude the
# originals here, was proposed and committed but then EXPLICITLY WITHDRAWN -- the original
# SVG artwork and rendering pipeline were previously approved and locked after extensive
# calibration/rendering work, and changing their format was not authorized. Both the asset
# references and this exclusion were reverted in the corrective commit. assets/creatures/
# and assets/relics/ are LOCKED: do not exclude any file under them, do not change what
# creature-test-assets.ts / relic-assets.ts require(), without explicit approval first.
#
# assets/creatures_old/ below is DIFFERENT from all of the above: it was never git-tracked at all
# (confirmed untracked, ~233MB, the single largest asset tree in the project) and was the one
# remaining thing the removed obsolete tree referenced. Excluded from the EAS upload here
# (EAS Build includes untracked-but-not-gitignored files by default) rather than deleted from
# disk, since it has no git history to recover it from -- that decision is left to whoever owns
# this local checkout.
assets/creatures_old/

# "assets/creatures copy/" (Work Package 3 audit): confirmed via SHA-1 checksum, file-for-file
# identical to assets/creatures_old/ above (69/69 files, same relative paths, same hashes) --
# an exact duplicate of the tree already excluded on the previous line. Also untracked, also
# unreferenced by any src/ import. Excluded for the same reason and under the same no-delete
# constraint as assets/creatures_old/.
assets/creatures copy/

# Documentation -- never imported by app code.
docs/
*.md

# Validation/QA scripts -- run via `npx tsx scripts/validate-*.ts` locally/in CI, never
# imported by the Expo app itself.
scripts/

# Supabase project (migrations, Edge Functions, local config) -- deployed separately via the
# Supabase CLI; never part of the Expo client bundle.
supabase/

# Standard non-build-artifact excludes.
.git/
.vscode/

# SECURITY (Work Package 3, Phase B Stage 2): because .easignore exists at all, EAS Build
# ignores .gitignore ENTIRELY for archive purposes (confirmed by reading eas-cli's own
# Ignore class, vcs/local.js -- ".gitignore files are not used" once a project has an
# .easignore). That means .gitignore's own `.env*` / `!.env.example` protection was NOT
# actually in effect for the EAS upload -- every local env file, including .env.local
# (which holds non-public credentials such as VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN alongside EXPO_PUBLIC_*
# values that are already meant to ship in the client bundle), was being uploaded in full
# with every build. Mirrored here so local secrets never enter the archive again. Production
# builds get their actual EXPO_PUBLIC_* / SENTRY_* config from EAS's own Environment
# Variables system (`eas env:list`), not from this file, so excluding it does not remove any
# configuration a real build needs.
.env*
!.env.example
